\newlabel{ch:08}{{5}{78}{Claim: Simple Trading Rules Beat Buy-and-Hold}{chapter.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces The moving-average rule applied to a simulated price. The rule holds the asset (shaded) while a short average of the price (5 days) is above a long one (150 days). It rides the long rise and avoids most of the fall, but it acts only after each turn has begun.}}{78}{figure.caption.21}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:06-ma-rule}{{5.1}{78}{The moving-average rule applied to a simulated price. The rule holds the asset (shaded) while a short average of the price (5 days) is above a long one (150 days). It rides the long rise and avoids most of the fall, but it acts only after each turn has begun}{figure.caption.21}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces The breakout rule applied to the same simulated price as in Figure~\ref {fig:06-ma-rule}. The rule buys when the price rises above its highest level of the previous 50 days and sells when it falls below its lowest level; shaded periods are the days it holds the asset. Like the moving-average rule, it acts only after each turn has begun, but in this example it reacts sooner.}}{79}{figure.caption.22}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:06-breakout-rule}{{5.2}{79}{The breakout rule applied to the same simulated price as in Figure~\ref {fig:06-ma-rule}. The rule buys when the price rises above its highest level of the previous 50 days and sells when it falls below its lowest level; shaded periods are the days it holds the asset. Like the moving-average rule, it acts only after each turn has begun, but in this example it reacts sooner}{figure.caption.22}{}}
